ities for \\(L_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  \\(L_{0}\\) is vertical:  \\(x=c\\)\n\nBecause the points of \\(T_{3}\\) have \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,2,3\\), we have \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  Then \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Any two of these three points determine a line whose slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\)).  \n  Consequently none of the two sunny lines can pass through any pair of the remaining points, so they cannot cover the three points. Contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  Then \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The points left uncovered are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  List all slopes of lines through two of these four points:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only **sunny** slope among them is \\(-\\tfrac12\\), realized by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Hence one sunny line can cover exactly these two points.  \n  The two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) are left; any line through them is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), which is non‑sunny.  \n  Thus the second sunny line cannot cover both, a contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  Then \\(L_{0}\\) contains only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The five remaining points must be covered by the two sunny lines.  \n  A sunny line can contain at most two of the points (any three of the five are not collinear with a sunny slope).  \n  Hence at most four points can be covered, contradicting the need to cover five.\n\nTherefore a vertical non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------