ry point of \\(T_{3}\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(L_i\\);  \n* exactly two of the \\(L_i\\) are sunny.  \n\nHence one line, say \\(L_{0}\\), is non\u2011sunny; it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWe examine all possibilities for \\(L_{0}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(L_{0}\\) is vertical:  \\(x=c\\)\n\nSince the points of \\(T_{3}\\) have \\(x\\)-coordinates \\(1,2,3\\), we must have \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  Then \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Any line through two of these three points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\)).  \n  All these slopes are non\u2011sunny, so the two sunny lines cannot cover the three points. Contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  Then \\(L_{0}\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  Computing slopes of all pairs:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,2)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only sunny slope among them is \\(-\\tfrac12\\), realized by the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Hence one sunny line can cover exactly these two points.  \n  The two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) are left; any line through them is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n  Thus the second sunny line cannot cover both, a contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  Then \\(L_{0}\\) contains only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The five remaining points must be covered by the two sunny lines.  \n  In the set \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\) any three points are never collinear with a sunny slope; the only possible sunny slopes are \\(1\\) (line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\)) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), but \\((3,1)\\) is already used).  \n  Consequently each sunny line can contain at most two of the five points, so at most four points can be covered\u2014insufficient. Contradiction.\n\nThus a vertical non\u2011sunny line cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L_{0}\\) is horizontal:  \\(y=d\\)\n\nThe situation is symmetric to the vertical case (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)).  \nFor \\(d=1,2,3\\) the same contradictions arise.  \nHence a horizontal non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(L_{0}\\) is diagonal:  \\(x+y=s\\)\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le4\\) for points of \\(T_{3}\\), we have \\(s\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(s=2\\).**  The line \\(x+y=2\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The other five points must be covered by two sunny lines, which is impossible by the same counting argument as in the vertical case \\(c=3\\).\n\n* **\\(s=3\\).**  The line \\(x+y=3\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Their pairwise slopes are\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty\\\\\n  (1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n  (1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n  (2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only sunny slope is \\(1\\), realized by the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n  After using this line, the points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain; any line through them has slope \\(-1\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n  Hence the second sunny line cannot cover both, contradiction.\n\n* **\\(s=4\\).**  The line \\(x+y=4\\) contains three points: \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Any two of these