us T_{m-1}\\) consists of the “outer layer”\n\n   \\[\n   L_m=\\{(a,m+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(m\\) points forming a broken line whose consecutive points have slopes \\(-1\\).  \n   Pair the points of \\(L_m\\) as  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,m),\\;(2,m-1);\\;(3,m-2),\\;(4,m-3);\\dots\n   \\]\n\n   (the last pair is \\((m-1,2),(m,1)\\) when \\(m\\) is even, and the middle point \\((\\frac{m+1}{2},\\frac{m+1}{2})\\) remains alone when \\(m\\) is odd).\n\n   * For each pair \\((a,m+1-a),(a+1,m-a)\\) we draw the line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_a:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{a}\\,x + \\frac{m+1}{a},\n     \\]\n\n     whose slope is \\(-\\frac1a\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.  \n   * If \\(m\\) is odd, the central point \\((\\frac{m+1}{2},\\frac{m+1}{2})\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x\\), which we add to the family.\n\n   The newly introduced lines are all distinct, sunny, and together they cover the whole outer layer \\(L_m\\).  \n   Adding them to the inductive family \\(\\mathcal C_{m-1}\\) yields a covering of \\(T_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n5. **From the all‑sunny covering to arbitrary \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\ge 3\\).  \n   Take the all‑sunny covering of \\(T_k\\) (proved above) and keep it unchanged.  \n   For the remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(T_n\\setminus T_k\\) use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n   Altogether we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny (horizontal) lines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   This construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* and *\\(k=1\\)* are possible (step 2).  \n   *\\(k=2\\)* is impossible (step 3).  \n   *\\(k\\ge 3\\)* are possible (steps 4–5).  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ and only these }k\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]"
