            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Choose the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n  All lines are vertical, therefore non‑sunny, and exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines are used.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n  \\]\n\n  and the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=x-(n-1) .\n  \\]\n\n  The vertical lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n  The only points not yet covered are \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\); both satisfy \\(y=x-(n-1)\\), so they lie on \\(L\\).  \n  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly one line (the line of slope \\(1\\)) is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}x+\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}.\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n  \\[\n  P_i=(n+1-i,\\;i),\\qquad Q_i=(n+2-i,\\;i-1),\n  \\]\n\n  both belonging to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n  \\[\n  (n+1-i)+i=n+1,\\qquad (n+2-i)+(i-1)=n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The slopes \\(\\frac{i}{n+1-i}\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\) and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); set \\(i=n+1-a\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n  Then \\(b\\le i\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\) because substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y=\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}a+\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}\n    =\\frac{i}{\\,a\\,}a+\\frac{i}{\\,a\\,}=i\\ge b .\n  \\]\n\n  A direct check shows that each \\((a,b)\\) indeed belongs to the appropriate \\(L_i\\).  \n  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nLet the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be non‑sunny.  \nSince the three families of non‑sunny lines are interchangeable by a rotation of the plane, we may replace each of them by a vertical line without affecting the covering property.  \nThus we may suppose that the non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nThe uncovered set is therefore  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n    =\\{(n-k+1,1),\\;(n-k+1,2),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.** *Any two distinct points of \\(S_k\\) determine a line whose slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in S_k\\) with \\((a,b