,\\\\\nL_4 &: y = 3x-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,2,3\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so all are sunny.  \nVerification:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point }(a,b) & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1\\;(1=1)\\\\\n(2,2) & L_1\\;(2=2)\\\\\n(1,2) & L_2\\;(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52)\\\\\n(3,1) & L_2\\;(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52)\\\\\n(1,3) & L_3\\;(3=2\\cdot1-1)\\\\\n(2,1) & L_4\\;(1=3\\cdot2-2)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nHence every point of \\(T_4\\) lies on at least one of the four sunny lines, and the configuration uses exactly \\(k=4=n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\nThese two lemmas give concrete families of lines that realise the extreme values \\(k=0\\) (for all \\(n\\ge3\\)) and \\(k=n\\) (for \\(n=3,4\\)). The general problem of characterising all admissible \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\) remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
