ever \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,1)=P_a\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n   If \\(b\\ge2\\) write \\(b=1+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n+1-b=n-r\\).  \n   Set \\(i=a\\). Then \\(Q_i=(i+1,2)=(a+1,2)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and the line \\(L_i\\) contains the whole arithmetic progression  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,1),\\;(a+1,2),\\;(a+2,3),\\dots ,\\;(a+r,\\,r+1) .\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the point \\((a,b)=(a,1+r)\\) is the \\((r+1)\\)-st point of this progression, hence it lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \n   Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (intermediate values).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   We construct a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 1 – choose \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Take the first \\(k\\) lines of the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) defined in (1); denote them \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \n   By Lemma 2 they are sunny and distinct.\n\n   *Step 2 – cover the remaining points.*  \n   Remove from \\(T_n\\) all points already covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines; call the remainder \\(U\\).  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) that does **not** appear as the first coordinate of a point of \\(U\\) we place the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_a:\\;x=a .\n   \\]\n\n   Because a vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, it is **not** sunny, as required.  \n   The set \\(\\{V_a\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines: there are \\(n\\) possible values of \\(a\\), and each sunny line \\(S_i\\) already uses the column \\(a=i\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) columns give the required number of vertical lines.\n\n   *Step 3 – verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n   * If \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) we are done.  \n   * Otherwise \\((a,b)\\) is not covered by any \\(S_i\\). By construction the column \\(a\\) is not used by a sunny line, hence the vertical line \\(V_a\\) is present in the family and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines or one of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  \n   The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), they are pairwise distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining Lemmas 1–3 we have proved that for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n   \\]\n\n   No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the definition of \\(k\\) already forces \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines).  \n\n   Therefore the problem is completely solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
