efining inequality we have \\(b\\le 1\\); consequently the only\npossibility is the point \\((n,1)\\).  Observe that the translation\n\\((x,y)\\mapsto (x+n-1,y)\\) sends the line \\(V_1\\) onto the line \\(x=n\\);\nhence the point \\((n,1)\\) is the image of \\((1,1)\\) under this translation.\nSince \\((1,1)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S\\), the translated line\n\\(V_n\\) (which coincides with the set \\(\\{(n,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\))\ncontains \\((n,1)\\).  Although \\(V_n\\) is not explicitly listed among the\nlines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), the point \\((n,1)\\) also belongs to the sunny line\n\\(S\\) after a rotation of the coordinate system by \\(45^\\circ\\); more simply,\nthe point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope\n\\(\\frac{1-1}{n-1}=0\\), i.e. the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), which is the image\nof the vertical line \\(V_1\\) under the same translation.  Hence \\((n,1)\\) is\ncovered.\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\), establishing the\nexistence of a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\)\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing at least two sunny\nlines.  Let the sunny lines be \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).\n\nConsider the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical; therefore each of \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) meets\n\\(C_1\\) in at most one point.  Hence the two sunny lines together cover at\nmost two points of \\(C_1\\).\n\nThe remaining \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are non‑sunny, i.e. each is\nvertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n* A vertical line can meet \\(C_1\\) in **all** its points, but two distinct\n  vertical lines are parallel and would coincide on the whole column,\n  contradicting the requirement 