            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nConsider the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Every line that is not vertical meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point*,\nbecause two distinct points of \\(C_1\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.\nHence each sunny line (which is never vertical) contributes at most one\npoint of \\(C_1\\).\n\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains **fewer than two non‑sunny lines**, then at most one\nline of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can be vertical.  Consequently the points of \\(C_1\\) can be\ncovered only in the following ways:\n\n* either the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is present, covering all \\(n\\) points of\n  \\(C_1\\);\n* or the vertical line is absent, in which case each of the \\(n\\) points of\n  \\(C_1\\) must be covered by a distinct line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe second alternative would require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving no\nline for the remaining points of \\(T_n\\); therefore it is impossible.\nThus the vertical line \\(x=1\\) **must** belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nApply the same reasoning to the first row  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA non‑horizontal line meets \\(R_1\\) in at most one point, and a sunny line is\nnever horizontal.  Hence, unless the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is present, we would\nagain need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover \\(R_1\\).  Consequently the\nhorizontal line \\(y=1\\) also belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nWe have shown that **both** \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are forced to be members of any\nadmissible family.  These two lines are non‑sunny, so at most the remaining\n\\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n##### 2.1  The basic non‑sunny configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1,\\qquad H:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{4,5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nA_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{V,H\\}\\cup\\{A_c\\mid c=4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; all of them are non‑sunny.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) falls into one of three categories.\n\n1. **\\(a=1\\).**  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V\\).\n2. **\\(b=1\\).**  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H\\).\n3. **\\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).**  \n   The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies\n   \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the antidiagonal \\(A_c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) uses no sunny\nlines, i.e. \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n##### 2.2  Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect a subset \\(D\\subset\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(|D|=k\\); let\n\\(C=\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus D\\).\n\nFor each \\(c\\in D\\) define a sunny line\n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;y-2=2\\bigl(x-(c-2)\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((c-2,2)\\) satisfies \\((c-2)+2=c\\); therefore it belongs to the\nantidiagonal \\(A_c\\) and consequently to \\(S_c\\).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\{V,H\\}\\cup\\bigl(\\{A_c\\mid c\\in C\\}\\cup\\{S_c\\mid c\\in D\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll antidiagonals \\(A_c\\) are distinct.  The points \\((c-2,2)\\) for different\n\\(c\\) are distinct, hence the sunny lines \\(S_c\\) are distinct from each other\nand from every antidiagonal (different slopes).  None of the \\(S_c\\) coincides\nwith \\(V\\) or \\(H\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_c\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) has \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(V\\);  \n* if \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\);  \n* otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(c:=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n\n  – If \\(c\\in C\\) then \\((a,b)\\in A_c\\subset\\mathcal F_k\\).  \n  – If \\(c\\in D\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the antidiagonal \\(A_c\\), and the\n    sunny line \\(S_c\\) also passes through the point \\((c-2,2)\\) of that\n    antidiagonal; consequently \\((a,b)\\) is covered by \\(S_c\\) (or by\n    \\(A_c\\) if we prefer).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nThe upper bound (1) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\)\nsunny lines.  The constructions of §2 demonstrate that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of all\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-2\\;}.\n\\]\n\nThis completes the determination of all possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
