            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** when its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it\nis **non‑sunny** (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\), hence is non‑sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(s=a+b\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are\ncovered and exactly \\(k=0\\) lines are sunny.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is possible for every }n\\ge3}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.** *If \\(n\\ge4\\) then any sunny line contains at most two points of\n\\(T_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\neq\\infty\\).\nAssume it contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\) with\n\\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  Collinearity gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\nb_3-b_1=m\\,(a_3-a_1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause each point satisfies \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n(a_3-a_1)+(b_3-b_1)\\le (n+1)-(a_1+b_1)\\le n . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (1) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n(1+m)(a_3-a_1)\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m\\) of a line through two lattice points is rational; after clearing\ndenominators we may assume \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Since \\(m\\neq-1\\), the factor\n\\(1+m\\) is a non‑zero integer, and for \\(n\\ge4\\) the inequality forces\n\\(a_3-a_1\\le1\\), contradicting \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  Hence no sunny line can contain\nthree points of \\(T_n\\). ∎  \n\nConsequently a sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most\n\\(2n\\) by Lemma 1.  Yet  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge4 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(k=n\\) for \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;y=\\tfrac12x\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none is parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\), and each point\nof \\(T_3\\) lies on one of them.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=n\\text{ is attainable exactly for }n=3}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Summary of proved results  \n\n*   For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n*   For \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  \n*   For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is **not** attainable.  \n\nThese statements constitute a rigorous partial solution of the original\nproblem.  The determination of the remaining admissible values of \\(k\\) (the\ninterval \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)) is left open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
